%T A400474 174,192,208,224,240,262,278,300,320,340,360,386,406,432,456,480,504,
%U A400474 534,558,588,616,644,672,706,734,768,800,832,864,902,934,972,1008,1044
%N A400474 Maximum, over all triangles ABC, of the number of points P strictly inside ABC such that the angles APB, BPC, CPA are pairwise distinct integer multiples of Pi/n.
%C A400474 This sequence arose from the following problem posed by the author: find the maximum number of points P strictly inside a triangle ABC such that the angles APB, BPC, CPA, measured in degrees, are integers and pairwise distinct. This is the case n = 180, and the answer is a(180) = 10562 (computed directly). Perhaps surprisingly, the equilateral triangle is not optimal: it gives only 10446, while a triangle with angles 59.7, 59.7, 60.6 degrees attains 10562. If the angles of ABC are also required to be integers, the maximum is 10446, attained only by the equilateral triangle (proved by writing the count for acute triangles as 10446 - (S + k)/2, where S is the sum of the squares of the deviations of the angles from 60 degrees and k is the number of odd angles, and checking that non-acute triangles give at most 9946; also verified by exhaustive search).
%C A400474 In general, the angles of the triangle ABC may be arbitrary real numbers.
%C A400474 For a point P inside ABC, the angles x = BPC, y = CPA, z = APB satisfy x + y + z = 2*Pi and A < x < Pi, B < y < Pi, C < z < Pi. Conversely, every such triple (x, y, z) corresponds to exactly one interior point P. Proof sketch: uniqueness holds because the circle through B and C on which BPC = x and the circle through C and A on which CPA = y meet in at most one point other than C; existence: on the part of the arc {APB = z} inside the triangle, the angle BPC varies continuously between max(A, Pi - z) and min(Pi, 2*Pi - z - B), and the conditions on (x, y, z) put x strictly between these values. Hence the count depends only on the integer parts of the angles of ABC measured in units of Pi/n.
%C A400474 All terms are even. Proof: with u = n - x/(Pi/n) etc., the counted triples are the ordered triples of distinct positive integers with u + v + w = n, minus the sets {u >= n - floor(A)}, {v >= n - floor(B)}, {w >= n - floor(C)} (A, B, C in units of Pi/n). These three sets are pairwise disjoint, and each is mapped to itself by swapping the other two coordinates, which has no fixed points since those coordinates are distinct. The total number of triples is 6 times the number of partitions of n into 3 distinct parts (= 6*A001399(n-6)).
%H A400474 Karan Sharma, <a href="/A400474/b400474.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 3..1000</a>
%F A400474 Conjecture: a(n) = (n^2 - 4*n + c)/3, where c = 6, -3, 4, 3, 0, -5 according as n == 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 (mod 6). Verified for n <= 1000.
%e A400474 a(6) = 6: with angles measured in multiples of 30 degrees, the angles APB, BPC, CPA must be 90, 120 and 150 degrees in some order. For the equilateral triangle, each of the 6 orders gives one point P.
%e A400474 a(9) = 16: here the angles are multiples of 20 degrees, and with u = 9 - x/(Pi/9) etc. there are 18 ordered triples of distinct positive integers with u + v + w = 9, coming from {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, {2,3,4}. For a triangle whose angles have integer parts 2, 2, 3 in units of 20 degrees (e.g., 58, 58, 64 degrees), only the 2 triples with w = 6 are excluded, giving 16.
%o A400474 (Python)
%o A400474 def a(n):
%o A400474     def cnt(p, q, r):
%o A400474         t = 0
%o A400474         for x in range(p + 1, n):
%o A400474             for y in range(q + 1, n):
%o A400474                 z = 2*n - x - y
%o A400474                 if r < z < n and x != y and y != z and x != z:
%o A400474                     t += 1
%o A400474         return t
%o A400474     best = 0
%o A400474     for s in (n - 2, n - 1, n):
%o A400474         m = 0 if s < n else 1
%o A400474         for p in range(m, n):
%o A400474             for q in range(m, n):
%o A400474                 r = s - p - q
%o A400474                 if r >= m:
%o A400474                     best = max(best, cnt(p, q, r))
%o A400474     return best
%Y A400474 Cf. A001399.
%K A400474 nonn,new
